That and most of your list aren't really game goals.  The game's one
defined goal is ascension, so you really at most need to measure:

Got the Bell?
Got the Book?
Got the Candelabrum?
Got the Amulet?

Now, you do have to descend to those points, so marking interim steps
(Reached the Oracle?  Medusa?  Castle?  Valley?) might be workable and
would give some idea of how far into the game a newer player is
getting.

Dive for Victory obviously lets you dive deeper faster, but the Castle
is non-diggable so at least reaching the Valley is a decent marker of
progress.

Basically, measure goals that actually move you directly toward win
the game.  Thoroughly exploring levels?  Why bother?  Once I'm kitted
out, I'm aiming to collect the invocation items, get the amulet, and
get out--I'm not going to explore every level thoroughly.  Sure, it's
a good idea early on, but only as a side goal--I've played games where
I didn't thoroughly explore anything below XL2 or so, letting me
ascend in 18,000 turns.  Rast regularly does things in half that
time.  I'm not saying to mark Rast's strategy as somehow better than
an extinctionist, I'm just saying that what ultimately matters is the
things that you need to do to ascend.  So measure those.



